  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This open-access scholarly book is showing early but concrete reach: it has been downloaded 926 times and has received 1 citation to date. Its strongest signal is usage, suggesting that the work is already circulating among readers interested in migration, colonial societies, and questions of belonging and inequality.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Leuven University Press in 2022, Comunidad, pertenencia, extranjer\u00eda examines labor and mercantile migration from the North Sea region to New Spain and argues that these migrants played a strategic role in the expansion and maintenance of the Hispanic monarchy. The book sits across political science, humanities, and philosophy, with thematic alignment to Reduced inequalities, indicating relevance not only for historical scholarship but also for broader discussions of mobility, exclusion, and social difference.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The clearest impact signal in the record is readership. OAPEN download metrics show sustained use, with a total of 926 downloads and noticeable month-to-month variation that includes a stronger peak in March. Because the book is openly available, this usage suggests accessible circulation beyond a narrow specialist audience, although downloads do not by themselves indicate citation or classroom adoption.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Scholarly uptake is still early but present. OpenAlex records 1 citation, appearing in a 2025 book chapter published by Cambridge University Press eBooks. With only a single citation, the evidence is best read as an initial sign of engagement rather than a mature citation pattern. The cited work\u2019s thematic focus on migration and labor dynamics aligns well with the book\u2019s subject matter, reinforcing the book\u2019s relevance to current research on mobility and social formation.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book\u2019s open-access status, along with support from KU Leuven, the Linnaeus University fund for open access publications, and the LNU Centre for Concurrences in Colonial and Postcolonial Studies, likely helps explain its reach. The record also indicates a dataset citation signal, but it does not provide enough detail to characterize that use further. Overall, the evidence points to a work that is already being read and has begun to enter scholarly conversation, even though the citation base remains small.</p>",
